class:: PerlinNoise2D
summary:: Periodic 2D Perlin-noise/fBm path oscillator
categories:: UGens>Generators
related:: Classes/PerlinNoise, Guides/PerlinNoisePorts, Classes/LFNoise0, Classes/LFNoise1

Description::

PerlinNoise2D is a two-dimensional gradient Perlin-noise oscillator. Instead of reading a single 1D noise curve, it traces a wrapped path through a seamless 2D gradient-noise tile and returns the value at the current coordinate.

code::xfreq:: and code::yfreq:: advance independent normalized phases through the x and y axes. With code::yfreq:: set to 0, code::yphase:: selects a fixed horizontal slice through the 2D field. With both frequencies moving, the UGen scans a 2D path whose motion depends on the frequency ratio.

For each octave, the UGen hashes the integer lattice coordinate code::(x, y):: together with code::seed:: to make a deterministic 2D unit-gradient vector. It then computes dot products from the four surrounding lattice corners to the current point, interpolates first in x and then in y with Perlin's quintic fade curve, and sums the octaves as fBm. Each octave doubles the lattice density while code::persistence:: scales the next octave's amplitude.

The gradient hash wraps at each octave's period, so the value field is periodic over code::0..1:: in both axes. The maximum octave count is 32. Octave-count modulation is latched at a safe wrap point: if y is static, changes apply on the next x wrap; if both axes move, changes apply when both axes wrap in the same sample. This avoids inserting or removing octave components in the middle of a scanned tile.

The closest vanilla comparison is code::LFNoise1:: used as a smooth random modulator, but the match is loose: PerlinNoise2D scans a deterministic 2D periodic field with x/y coordinates and fBm octaves, while LFNoise1 generates a one-dimensional random breakpoint signal.

subsection:: Aliasing limit

This UGen is not oversampled and not band-limited. With lacunarity fixed at 2, the highest lattice rate in either moving axis is approximately code::max(abs(xfreq), abs(yfreq)) * (2 ** (octaves - 1))::. A conservative useful-octave limit is:

code::
maxSafeOctaves = floor(log2(SampleRate.ir * 0.5 / max(abs(xfreq), abs(yfreq)))) + 1
::

Requested octaves above that limit can fold back as aliases. The real audible limit may be lower because the 2D interpolation path can create extra high-frequency motion, especially with fast x/y motion and high code::persistence::.

classmethods::

method::ar

argument::xfreq
X-axis phase frequency in Hz. Audio-rate modulation is supported. Values are clipped to code::-SampleRate * 0.499..SampleRate * 0.499::.

argument::yfreq
Y-axis phase frequency in Hz. Audio-rate modulation is supported. Use code::0:: for a fixed y slice.

argument::xphase
Initial x coordinate in cycles, wrapped to code::0..1::.

argument::yphase
Initial y coordinate in cycles, wrapped to code::0..1::.

argument::octaves
Number of fBm octaves, rounded to the nearest integer and clipped to code::1..32::. Changes are latched at safe wrap points.

argument::persistence
Per-octave amplitude multiplier, clipped to code::0..1::.

argument::seed
Integer-like deterministic gradient seed.

argument::mul
Output multiplier.

argument::add
Output offset.

